STS is a security token service that provide a APIs to get a temporary security token to perform operation on cloud.
It is used with certain role that can be assumnd by any other IAM role, IAM User, aws Service, SAML user, federated user erc.
Magically withh this you can perform operation on AWS without any actual IAM user or direct IAM role. with just a fzcebook id and a assumable IAM role i can
do operation on AWS.

APIs of STS:Assume* always return  below items
    {
      "Credentials": {
        "AccessKeyId": "ASIA...",
        "SecretAccessKey": "...",
        "SessionToken": "...",
        "Expiration": "2026-07-21T..."
      }
    }
These are short lived token that can be used for AWS operations.

STS:AssumeRole  :-
  This one provide the access tokes for any other IAM user, IAM role or AWS service. with IAM user "mufazzal" you can assume role "r1" and perform anu operation that r1 allow to do.
  DO note that IAM user "mufazzal" must have permission to call STS:AssumeRole
CMD to assume role:-
  aws sts assume-role --role-arn "arn:aws:iam::637460932892:role/vendor1-cross-account-role" --role-session-name "vendor-x-access" --external-id "iammufvendor1" --profile muf_personal_vendor1
  Passing external id add a check to make sure correct user is using it. this check should also be there in Condition of trust policy of Role "r1"


How to use this keys and token:-
    1. You can set it to env variable like
            $env:AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID = "ASIA..."
            $env:AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY = "..."
            $env:AWS_SESSION_TOKEN = "..."
    2. Set it in .aws/credentia file
        [my_asumed_role]
            aws_access_key_id=..
            aws_secret_access_key=...
            aws_session_token="........"

The token is valid for certail amount of timer only. For this time frame you are allowed to use this keys to perform allowed ops on cloud. after that it will not be allowed
in --role-session-name you can set the idenfifiable name for this time frame. 

--role-session-name:-
When you assume the role and get the keys, now when you use this keys to perform any operation on cloud, the value passed in '--role-session-name' is considered as a end user name who perform the ops.
this is how cloudtrail log look like:-
    "userIdentity": {
            "type": "AssumedRole",
            "principalId": "AROAZI25B5EOFB7THQNUW:vendor-x-access",
            "arn": "arn:aws:sts::637460932892:assumed-role/vendor1-cross-account-role/vendor-x-access",    
            "sessionIssuer": {
                "type": "Role",
                "principalId": "AROAZI25B5EOFB7THQNUW",
                "arn": "arn:aws:iam::637460932892:role/vendor1-cross-account-role",
                "accountId": "637460932892",
                "userName": "vendor1-cross-account-role"
            },
    }


Assume role and Console access:-
    Whenever a trust polciy is created that allow an IAM user of any aws account to assume it. By default a aws console access also provided to that IAM user via "Switch Role" button of aws console.
    Ex:-
        Suppose "home" aws account has a role "for_vendor" that can be assumed by "vendor1" aws account [or any specifi IAM role of "vendor1" aws account ]. 
        Now whenever IAM user of "vendor1" loggin to "vendor1" aws account he can swich role and provide below detail in for:
            1. Account id of "home" aws account
            2. role name  provided by "home" aws account [for_vendor in this case]
            press ok, and thw IAM user of "vendor1" aws account sniffed in "home" aws account with the role of "for_vendor"
            of course accesibilty of that aws console is limited via policy of "for_vendor"
